	Software Necessary
Microcontroller Programmer: arduino-ide_2.3.10_Linux_64bit.AppImage
I'm not sure what this does: encoder.jar
Ducky Code to .ino translator: duck2spark3.py

	Programming Instructions:
Step 0: download and configure arduino-ide
	visit
https://sourceforge.net/projects/arduino-ide.mirror/files/2.3.10/arduino-ide_2.3.10_Linux_64bit.AppImage/download
	and download the app image. Run it and update if necessary

	Add the board manager we need to use by pressing CTRL + , or going to File->Preferences. Find 'Additional Board Manager URLs" at the bottom and paste this link:
	https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ArminJo/DigistumpArduino/master/package_digistump_index.json

	Go to the Board manager on the left and search for "Digistump AVR Boards" and install it. Then go to Tools->Board->Digistump AVR Boards and select DigiSpark from the dropdown. Finally, go to Tools->Programmer and select "micronucleus.2.5" from the dropdown.

Step 1: design .duck script
	echo -e "REM example\nDELAY 1000\nSTRING Did I type this?" > example.duck
Step 2: encode as binary
	java -jar encoder.jar -i script.duck -o script_raw.bin

Step 3: translate binary .duck script to .ino
	./duck2spark3.py -i script_raw.bin -f 1 -o raw.ino

Step 4: make working directory for Arduino IDE to use and move .ino
	mkdir raw; mv raw.ino raw;

Step 5: run Arduino IDE with .ino as input file
	./arduino-ide_2.3.10_Linux_64bit.AppImage ./raw/raw.ino

Step 6: cleanup intermediary files, keeping the easily edited .duck
	rm -r raw/ script_raw.bin	

This is automated by complile&execute.sh, which takes the .duck filename and runs it through all these steps. 
Note that complile&execute.sh is expecting arduino-ide*.AppImage, duck2spark3.py, and encoder.jar to all exist in the folder where it is run.
